centre point $x_0$ is included.","The radius $r$ is negative.","The set contains no real numbers."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"27ceb319fb4b2633ce316d11"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_punctured_neighbourhood_4c3ae978_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which set is a punctured neighbourhood of $0$?","options":["$[-1,1]$.","$(-1,0)\\cup(0,1)$.","$\\{0\\}$.","$[0,1)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6167cc3a2602584507b40c67"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_punctured_neighbourhood_4c3ae978_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $x_0 = 2$ and $r = 1$, which point belongs to the punctured neighbourhood?","options":["$\\frac{5}{2}$.","$2$.","$3$.","$0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fa218c5372fc9435217624a9"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_punctured_neighbourhood_4c3ae978_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why are punctured neighbourhoods used in definitions of limits?","options":["They describe points close to the centre while excluding the centre value itself.","They force the function to be continuous at the centre.","They require the function value at the centre to equal $0$, regardless of the specific input chosen.","They include only points far away from the centre."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"41f4e15519baf4c2ea6c873d"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_punctured_neighbourhood_4c3ae978_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What must the radius $r$ of a punctured neighbourhood satisfy?","options":["The radius must satisfy $r = 0$.","The radius must satisfy $r \\lt 0$.","The radius may be omitted from the definition.","The radius must satisfy $r \\gt 0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"665791632d674c555cffb9a9"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_punctured_neighbourhood_4c3ae978_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Write the punctured neighbourhood of $2$ with radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ as intervals.","options":["$[\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}]$.","$(2,\\frac{5}{2})$ only.","$(\\frac{3}{2},2)\\cup(2,\\frac{5}{2})$.","$(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5d6814fad4489e05e5b753a1"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_punctured_neighbourhood_4c3ae978_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which point is excluded from every punctured neighbourhood of $x_0$?","options":["$x_0+r$.","$x_0-r$.","$0$ regardless of $x_0$.","$x_0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"356eac1844087b04056bb4de"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_punctured_neighbourhood_4c3ae978_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which missing assumption would make $\\{x\\in\\mathbb{R}:0 \\lt |x-x_0| \\lt r\\}$ fail to be a punctured neighbourhood as defined?","options":["$x_0$ is the largest real number.","$x_0$ is an integer.","$r \\gt 0$.","$r$ is written as a fraction."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"42668b0dabde553546579348"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_punctured_neighbourhood_4c3ae978_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does a punctured neighbourhood of $a$ exclude that an ordinary neighbourhood keeps?","options":["The punctured neighbourhood excludes all nearby points.","The punctured neighbourhood can only be centred at $0$.","The punctured neighbourhood must be a finite set.","The punctured neighbourhood excludes the centre point."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ad3e526d1bd288328f4fe650"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_punctured_neighbourhood_4c3ae978_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $S = \\{x\\in\\mathbb{R}:0 \\lt |x-3| \\lt 2\\}$. What is $S$?","options":["$(1,5)$.","$[1,5]$.","$(1,3)\\cup(3,5)$.","$\\{3\\}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c65ca8ae5d10b04e055dca63"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_punctured_neighbourhood_4c3ae978_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A function is not defined at $x_0$ but is defined on a punctured neighbourhood of $x_0$. Which concept can still be studied safely using that neighbourhood?","options":["The value $f(x_0)$.","The limit of the function at $x_0$.","Whether $f(x_0)$ is positive.","The exact coefficient of a polynomial at $x_0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d72f840027f502ff4b77d6fe"},{"id":"punctured_neighbourhood_new_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does the word punctured indicate in a punctured neighbourhood?","options":["The radius is zero","The set is empty","Only endpoints are included","The centre point is removed"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"58cc9e93573c925de61c4f71"},{"id":"punctured_neighbourhood_new_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does $|x-x_0| \\lt r$ express?","options":["$x$ is within distance $r$ of $x_0$","$x$ equals $x_0$","$x$ is farther than $r$ from $x_0$","$r$ is negative"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f962e3e8c5a33e3f3f886472"},{"id":"punctured_neighbourhood_new_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the definition require $r \\gt 0$?","options":["To give a genuine neighbourhood around $x_0$","To force the set to contain only $x_0$","To make $|x-x_0| \\lt r$ impossible","To make $x_0$ negative"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3824c5f8bcfe98967721dd5c"},{"id":"punctured_neighbourhood_new_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $x_0=0$ and $r=2$, which set is the punctured neighbourhood?","options":["$[-2,2]$","$\\{0\\}$","$(-2,0)\\cup(0,2)$","$(0,2)$ only"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"805cb4e2ea84dc42bd4e353e"},{"id":"punctured_neighbourhood_new_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $x_0=3$ and $r=2$, which point is not in the punctured neighbourhood?","options":["$3$","$2$","$4$","$\\frac72$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e4e1745792a57aebe4903030"},{"id":"punctured_neighbourhood_new_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $x_0=1$ and $r=3$, which point belongs to the punctured neighbourhood?","options":["$-1$","$1$","$4$","$5$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b9c0955e57f16156844fefbf"},{"id":"punctured_neighbourhood_new_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why do punctured neighbourhoods avoid reliance on $f(x_0)$ in limits?","options":["They only use points near $x_0$ but not equal to $x_0$","They force $f(x_0)=0$","They include no nearby points","They require $f$ to be undefined everywhere"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8e50571ff87365848a9af17d"},{"id":"punctured_neighbourhood_new_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which interval expression equals $\\{x\\in\\mathbb R:0 \\lt |x+1| \\lt 2\\}$?","options":["$[-3,1]$","$(-1,1)$","$(-3,-1]$","$(-3,-1)\\cup(-1,1)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4d22a1def41706b1ac010645"}]}}